arch. cite21†Tools & services Explore our latest AI models and products. cite22†Open source Discover open-source code and collaborate with the community. L61: \nL62: cite30†Conferences & events L63: \nL64: Careers\nL65: ## Shaping the future together\nL66: \nL67: cite23†See all programs L68: \nL69: cite24†Faculty programs Participating in the academic research community through meaningful engagement with university faculty. cite25†Student programs Supporting the next generation of researchers through a wide range of programming. cite26†R&D programs Collaborating with industry and academic experts to support foundational science and innovation. cite27†Locations Find your place in our global offices and research labs. L70: \nL71: cite31†Blog L72: \nL73: About\nL74: ## Translating discovery into real-world impact\nL75: \nL76: cite28†People Our researchers drive advancements in computer science through both fundamental and applied research. cite29†Teams Collaborative groups tackling the world's most challenging AI problems. L77: \nL78: cite32†Google Research L79: \nL80: cite33†Google AI Learn about all our AI†ai.google cite34†Google DeepMind Explore the frontier of AI†deepmind.google cite35†Google Labs Try our AI experiments†labs.google L81: \nL82: [Button: Research ]\nL83: \nL84: [Button: Resources ]\nL85: \nL86: cite30†Conferences & events L87: [Button: Careers ]\nL88: \nL89: cite31†Blog L90: \nL91: [Button: About ]\nL92: \nL93: [Input: Search]\nL94: \nL95: Search\nL96: # RosettaTrace: Human interpretable protein sequence design using rollout-guided search with Large Language Models\nL97: \nL98: Fanglei Xue\nL99: \nL100: Rahul Dodhia\nL101: \nL102: David Baker\nL103: \nL104: Meghana Kshirsagar\nL105: \nL106: Frank DiMaio\nL107: \nL108: Juan Lavista Ferres\nL109: \nL110: Ching-An Cheng\nL111: \nL112: ArXiv (2026)\nL113: \nL114: cite36†Download†arxiv.org cite37†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L115: ## Abstract\nL116: We introduce RosettaTrace, an inference-time optimization approach that uses large language models as a policy within a rollout-based search algorithm, ranking candidate sequences using scalarized rewards computed from structure prediction models such as RosettaFold3. Unlike training-based sequence design methods, RosettaTrace performs optimization entirely at inference time and uses structured textual feedback.\nL117: Across 500 protein redesign tasks with low designability metrics, RosettaTrace obtains between 17 to 65\\% improvement in the metrics. When refining candidates generated by LigandMPNN, RosettaTrace achieves a 2× improvement in design success rates, and matches LigandMPNN when initialized from suboptimal native sequences. Finally, we show that rollout trajectories produced by RosettaTrace can be reused as preference data for continual model adaptation.\nL118: ×\n"
